Subject: [PATCH] drm/msm/dpu: enable the QSEED detail enhancer and direction filter

QSEED3/QSEED3Lite has a detail enhancer and a direction-detection filter, and
dpu_hw_util.c already knows how to program both: _dpu_hw_setup_scaler3_de()
writes the whole sharpen/shape/threshold/adjust register block, and the op_mode
bits for DE (bit 8) and dir_en (bit 4) are already wired up. Nothing ever sets
them. Every plane that scales on this hardware therefore gets a plain bilinear
resample and leaves the sharpening block dark.

Fill in the enhancer's default working point whenever QSEED is actually
resampling, and enable the direction filter alongside it. The threshold pair
(thr_quiet / thr_dieout) is what keeps this from being a blanket sharpen: flat
and noisy regions fall outside the linear zone and are left alone, so the
enhancer puts back edge detail a bilinear upscale loses rather than amplifying
grain. Coefficient LUT blobs stay a userspace concern; this only covers the
defaults the hardware ships with.

Two register writes were missing on the newer scaler revisions:

  - DE_LPF_BLEND (0x64), the low-pass blend weight between the enhanced and
    the source pixel, exists from QSEED revision 3.0 and was never written.
  - DIR_FILTER_WEIGHT (0x60) was only written from the QSEED3Lite LUT path,
    so a pipe with no userspace LUT blob ran the direction filter at whatever
    weight was left in the register. Write it for every 2.4-and-later scaler.

The DE_SHARPEN_CTL blend field (bits 20-23) also had no struct member, so add
one rather than leaving a hole in the register description.

Deliberately gated on src != dst. The extents compared here are already
post-rotation (_dpu_plane_setup_scaler3() swaps them at the top for an inline
rotated pipe), so a rotated 1:1 RGB pipe -- which has to keep the scaler
programmed as a pass-through -- correctly does not sharpen.

Authored by tiopex <tiopxyz@gmail.com>.

NOT UPSTREAM as of the 7.2.5 tree this is pinned to.

ON HARDWARE 2026-09-12, but only partly exercised -- be precise about which half.

Shipped in the image that Pocket ACE, Pocket FIT and Pocket S2 all booted during
the inline-rotation gate, with the display up and no regression, so the patch is
proven not to break a non-scaling pipe. The DIR_FILTER_WEIGHT write DID run:
every one of those pipes has a QSEED 3.2/3.3 scaler, so it is past the 0x2004
gate, and it wrote dir_weight = 0 on every atomic commit.

The DETAIL ENHANCER ITSELF never engaged, because nothing scaled. gamescope hands
the panel its native mode, and for a rotated pipe _dpu_plane_setup_scaler3()
compares the extents AFTER its rot90 swap: src 1920x1080 becomes 1080x1920, which
is exactly dst, so `src_w != dst_w || src_h != dst_h` was false throughout. The
enhancer stays dark until something actually resamples -- a game at a non-native
resolution reaching the plane without a compositor pass first.

So: the plumbing is HW-clean, the sharpening is unproven. When a scaling case does
appear, look for over-sharpening or ringing on upscaled content before trusting
the curve; these are the hardware's default values, not a tuned choice.

SM8250 NOW COVERED FOR THE NON-SCALING PATH, 2026-09-13, image 20260913T180307Z.
The gate above used Pocket ACE, Pocket FIT and Pocket S2 -- every one of them
SM8550 or SM8650 -- so nothing had exercised a 3.0 scaler. Pocket Max and AYN
Thor Lite have now both booted this patch with their displays up and no
regression. Both are QSEED 3.0 (dpu_vig_sblk_qseed3_3_0), so the DIR_FILTER_WEIGHT
write past the 0x2004 gate -- which runs on EVERY atomic commit, scaling or not --
is clean on 3.0 as well as on 3.2/3.3.

THE 3.0 CUTOFF FOR DE_LPF_BLEND IS AN ASSERTION, NOT A CITATION -- read this
before widening it. The `scaler_version >= 0x3000` gate says the register exists
from QSEED revision 3.0, and no source available to this tree confirms that: the
hardware documentation is not public, and the revision at which 0x64 becomes
valid could equally be 3.2. The claim has been disputed elsewhere, on the grounds
that a 3.0 scaler predates the register entirely. Nobody in either direction has
produced a datasheet.

What keeps that from being urgent is the placement: the write sits INSIDE
`if (de.enable)`, and de.enable is only set by the src != dst branch below. So on
a 3.0 part the contested register is untouched until something actually
resamples. The exposure is exactly "a scaling plane on SM8250".

THE SCALING CASE HAS NOW RUN ON A 3.0 SCALER -- POCKET MAX, 2026-09-13. At its
shipped settings that board never scales: the compositor renders at the panel's
own size and the live plane reads src-pos 1080x1920 against crtc-pos 1080x1920,
so the branch below is false and 0x64 is never written. That is a property of
the configuration, not of the hardware, and the compositor already carries the
flag that changes it -- the one that clamps the logical output on a rotated
panel and lets the plane upscale, used in production on the wide SM8650 panel.
Pointing it at 720 lines on Pocket Max gives

    logical output 1920x1080 -> 1280x720, plane upscales to the 1080x1920 mode
    plane[59]  src-pos = 720x1280    crtc-pos = 1080x1920   <- scaling, 1.5x
    plane[89]  src-pos = 1080x1920   crtc-pos = 1080x1920   <- 1:1, untouched

so de.enable, dir_en and dir_weight are all set on a QSEED 3.0 part and 0x64 IS
written. Result: dmesg clean -- no DPU errors, no underruns, no timeouts, no
wedge -- with the display up and scanning out the scaled plane. The two planes
above also show the src != dst gate discriminating correctly per-plane.

So the "3.0 predates DE_LPF_BLEND" worry does not show up as a safety problem on
3.0. Be precise about what that does and does not establish: a clean dmesg does
not prove the register is valid there, only that writing it breaks nothing. If
0x64 is reserved on 3.0 the write is simply inert, and the enhancer still runs
without its blend weight.

STILL OPEN, and it is the reason the patch is worth doubting at all: the CURVES.
They are the hardware defaults, not a tuned choice, and whether they ring or
over-sharpen on real upscaled content is an eyeball judgement nobody has made
yet. Reproduce with the flag above and look for halos on text edges and noise
amplification in flat gradients. If they are visible, tune sharpen_level1/2 and
thr_quiet/thr_dieout, or gate DE to >= 0x3200 and take the question off the
table for every 3.0 part.

If that case ever turns up and misbehaves, the cheap fix is to move the DE gate to
`>= 0x3200`. That excludes every 3.0 part and costs nothing on the boards the
sharpening was wanted for, which are all 3.2 or later. Do not widen the gate below
3.0 without a register map.
--- a/drivers/gpu/drm/msm/disp/dpu1/dpu_hw_util.h
+++ b/drivers/gpu/drm/msm/disp/dpu1/dpu_hw_util.h
@@ -66,6 +66,7 @@
  * @ adjust_a:      A-coefficients for mapping curve
  * @ adjust_b:      B-coefficients for mapping curve
  * @ adjust_c:      C-coefficients for mapping curve
+ * @ blend:         blend weight between the enhanced and the source pixel
  */
 struct dpu_hw_scaler3_de_cfg {
 	u32 enable;
@@ -81,6 +82,7 @@
 	int16_t adjust_a[DPU_MAX_DE_CURVES];
 	int16_t adjust_b[DPU_MAX_DE_CURVES];
 	int16_t adjust_c[DPU_MAX_DE_CURVES];
+	uint32_t blend;
 };
 
 
--- a/drivers/gpu/drm/msm/disp/dpu1/dpu_hw_util.c
+++ b/drivers/gpu/drm/msm/disp/dpu1/dpu_hw_util.c
@@ -67,6 +67,8 @@
 #define QSEED3LITE_COEF_LUT_CTRL              0x4C
 #define QSEED3LITE_COEF_LUT_SWAP_BIT          0
 #define QSEED3LITE_DIR_FILTER_WEIGHT          0x60
+#define QSEED3_DE_LPF_BLEND                   0x64
+#define QSEED3_DEFAULT_DE_LPF_BLEND    0x3FF00000
 #define QSEED3LITE_FILTERS                 2
 #define QSEED3LITE_SEPARABLE_LUTS             10
 #define QSEED3LITE_LUT_SIZE                   33
@@ -256,7 +258,8 @@
 
 	sharp_ctl = ((de_cfg->limit & 0xF) << 9) |
 		((de_cfg->prec_shift & 0x7) << 13) |
-		((de_cfg->clip & 0x7) << 16);
+		((de_cfg->clip & 0x7) << 16) |
+		((de_cfg->blend & 0xF) << 20);
 
 	shape_ctl = (de_cfg->thr_quiet & 0xFF) |
 		((de_cfg->thr_dieout & 0x3FF) << 16);
@@ -326,8 +329,15 @@
 	if (scaler3_cfg->de.enable) {
 		_dpu_hw_setup_scaler3_de(c, &scaler3_cfg->de, scaler_offset);
 		op_mode |= BIT(8);
+		if (scaler_version >= 0x3000)
+			DPU_REG_WRITE(c, QSEED3_DE_LPF_BLEND + scaler_offset,
+				      QSEED3_DEFAULT_DE_LPF_BLEND);
 	}
 
+	if (scaler_version >= 0x2004)
+		DPU_REG_WRITE(c, QSEED3LITE_DIR_FILTER_WEIGHT + scaler_offset,
+			      scaler3_cfg->dir_weight & 0xFF);
+
 	if (scaler3_cfg->lut_flag) {
 		if (scaler_version < 0x2004)
 			_dpu_hw_setup_scaler3_lut(c, scaler3_cfg, scaler_offset);
--- a/drivers/gpu/drm/msm/disp/dpu1/dpu_plane.c
+++ b/drivers/gpu/drm/msm/disp/dpu1/dpu_plane.c
@@ -492,6 +492,38 @@
 	scale_cfg->lut_flag = 0;
 	scale_cfg->blend_cfg = 1;
 	scale_cfg->enable = 1;
+
+	/*
+	 * The direction filter and the detail enhancer only earn their power
+	 * when QSEED is really resampling. A rotated 1:1 RGB pipe keeps the
+	 * scaler on as a pass-through (see above) and must not be sharpened.
+	 *
+	 * The curve below is the hardware's own default working point: the
+	 * enhancer adds back the high-frequency detail a bilinear upscale
+	 * loses, with thr_quiet/thr_dieout holding it off flat and noisy
+	 * regions so it sharpens edges rather than grain.
+	 */
+	if (src_w != dst_w || src_h != dst_h) {
+		scale_cfg->dir_en = 1;
+		scale_cfg->dir_weight = 32;
+
+		scale_cfg->de.enable = 1;
+		scale_cfg->de.sharpen_level1 = 8;
+		scale_cfg->de.sharpen_level2 = 8;
+		scale_cfg->de.clip = 0;
+		scale_cfg->de.limit = 4;
+		scale_cfg->de.thr_quiet = 8;
+		scale_cfg->de.thr_dieout = 256;
+		scale_cfg->de.thr_low = 8;
+		scale_cfg->de.thr_high = 64;
+		scale_cfg->de.prec_shift = 2;
+		scale_cfg->de.blend = 0;
+		for (i = 0; i < DPU_MAX_DE_CURVES; i++) {
+			scale_cfg->de.adjust_a[i] = 112;
+			scale_cfg->de.adjust_b[i] = 0;
+			scale_cfg->de.adjust_c[i] = 0;
+		}
+	}
 }
 
 static void _dpu_plane_setup_pixel_ext(struct dpu_hw_scaler3_cfg *scale_cfg,
